==Aftermath==
After the players had collected their medals, UEFA president [[Lennart Johansson]] handed over the trophy to [[Bülent Korkmaz]]. Korkmaz celebrated by raising the silverware, together with Şükür and the rest of the Galatasaray squad on the podium, including the ejected Hagi himself as golden confetti rained down.<ref>{{cite news |first=Türker |last=Tozar |title=Cup carries special resonance for Korkmaz |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01d4-0e7080da61e6-2e2d0909856d-1000--cup-carries-special-resonance-for-korkmaz/ |publisher=UEFA |date=3 December 2008 |access-date=29 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gözümde canlanır koskoca mazi.. |url=http://www.ajansspor.com/futbol/uefakupasi/h/20100511/gozumde_canlanir_koskoca_mazi.html |work=Ajansspor |date=11 May 2010 |access-date=29 July 2013 |language=tr |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140201203547/http://www.ajansspor.com/futbol/uefakupasi/h/20100511/gozumde_canlanir_koskoca_mazi.html |archive-date=1 February 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> A selected UEFA panel named Taffarel as the man of the match, and presented him with a trophy after the game.<ref name="man_of_the_match"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.collectsoccer.com/acatalog/Galatasaray-Arsenal-17.05.2000-UEFA.Final-PressPack.jpg |title=Press pack – Galatasaray – Arsenal |work=UEFA (Union of European Football Associations) |publisher=CollectSoccer |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=23 July 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131220141720/http://collectsoccer.com/acatalog/Galatasaray-Arsenal-17.05.2000-UEFA.Final-PressPack.jpg |archive-date=20 December 2013}}</ref>

Galatasaray manager [[Fatih Terim]] expressed his delight following his side's victory. He was interviewed by the Turkish press and dedicated the cup to Turkey and its public, particularly to those who lost their lives during the [[1999 İzmit earthquake]]: "Many people suffered terribly in the earthquake in Turkey last year and if this victory brings some happiness back into their lives then I am delighted and so are all the players. We are very proud if we are able to help the Turkish people in some small way and this victory is for the whole country and all of the soccer fans in Turkey. I believe they were all united behind us". Terim also praised his own players and Taffarel: "I am proud and delighted he is in my team. He was magnificent and thoroughly deserved to be man of the match". Popescu, a former defender of [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]], also indulged in the triumph and said: "I am sure the fans at my old club are delighted as well!"<ref>{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Bradley |title=Sad Gunners feel pain of Popescu's spurs; Galatasaray 0 Arsenal 0 (AET: Galatasaray win 4–1 on penalties) |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Sad+Gunners+feel+pain+of+Popescu's+spurs%3B+Galatasaray+0+Arsenal+0...-a062159560 |work=Birmingham Post |date=18 May 2000 |access-date=30 July 2013}}</ref>

As some Arsenal players were seen consoling each other,<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=Cole |url=http://www.gettyimages.co.uk/detail/news-photo/despair-for-marc-overmars-and-martin-keown-of-arsenal-after-news-photo/1038170 |title=Marc Overmars, Martin Keown |publisher=Getty Images |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=26 August 2014}}</ref> manager [[Arsène Wenger]] was left to rue the defeat and said that his Arsenal side could not take their chances: "It was not a huge advantage for us to have Hagi sent off, sometimes you defend better with 10 men because everybody is focused." He was displeased with the penalty shoot-out and criticised Spanish referee [[Antonio Jesús López Nieto|Antonio López Nieto]] for deciding the penalty shoot-out to take place in front of Galatasaray fans. Wenger was also unhappy with the decision made by UEFA officials regarding a coin toss during extra time, which would affect where the spot kicks would be taken.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger anger over shoot-out |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/753010.stm |work=BBC News |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=1 August 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Turkish delight as Gunners lose nerve |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/spl/aberdeen/turkish-delight-as-gunners-lose-nerve-1.235279 |work=The Herald |date=18 May 2000 |access-date=1 August 2013}}</ref>

The win was widely celebrated in Turkey; Galatasaray players and staff received a hero's welcome in [[Istanbul]] from the club's fans.<ref name="Galatasaray_victory">{{cite news |title=Turkey honors Galatasaray for UEFA Cup win |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/news/2000/05/19/galatasaray_medal_ap/ |work=CNN Sports Illustrated |publisher=Cable News Network |agency=Associated Press |date=19 May 2000 |access-date=29 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20010712234010/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/news/2000/05/19/galatasaray_medal_ap/ |archive-date=12 July 2001}}</ref> The Turkish media hailed the match as one of the best achievements in their sports history and the biggest in football, the country's most popular sport. The [[Ministry of Youth and Sports (Turkey)|Ministry of Youth and Sports]] minister {{ill|Fikret Ünlü|tr}}, who attended the final, described the performance as "marvelous" and "a big present from Galatasaray to Turkey".<ref>{{cite news |title=Turkish delight – Galatasaray wins UEFA Cup on penalty kicks |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/news/2000/05/17/uefa_final_ap/ |work=CNN Sports Illustrated |publisher=Cable News Network |agency=Associated Press |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=29 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080408204536/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/world/news/2000/05/17/uefa_final_ap/ |archive-date=8 April 2008}}</ref> [[Ahmet Necdet Sezer]], the President of Turkey highlighted the club's success by awarding the team with the [[State Medal of Distinguished Service (Turkey)|State Medal of Distinguished Service]], as a result for winning the country's first European competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Galatasaray to be awarded highest possible honors |url=http://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/default.aspx?pageid=438&n=galatasaray-to-be-awarded-highest-possible-honors-2000-05-19 |work=Hürriyet Daily News |date=19 May 2000 |access-date=29 July 2013}}</ref> In August 2013 the two sides met in an [[2013 Emirates Cup|Emirates Cup]] pre-season match. The friendly ended in a 2–1 win for Galatasaray.<ref>{{cite news |title=Emirates Cup returns for summer 2013 |url=http://www.arsenal.com/emiratescup |work=Arsenal F.C. official website |date=15 May 2013 |access-date=1 August 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130726182314/http://www.arsenal.com/emiratescup |archive-date=26 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Jim |last=Van Wijk |title=Arsenal 1 Galatasaray 2 match report: Didier Drogba returns to haunt Gunners as Turkish side win Emirates Cup |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsenal-1-galatasaray-2-match-report-didier-drogba-returns-to-haunt-gunners-as-turkish-side-win-emirates-cup-8745505.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsenal-1-galatasaray-2-match-report-didier-drogba-returns-to-haunt-gunners-as-turkish-side-win-emirates-cup-8745505.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |work=The Independent |date=4 August 2013 |access-date=7 August 2013}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

==Fan riots==
{{main|2000 UEFA Cup Final riots}}

[[File:Copenhagen aerial view by night.jpg|thumb|left|220px|The City Hall Square, where the riots took place]]

The final was overshadowed by [[2000 UEFA Cup Final riots|riots]] between the two sides – it began when Galatasaray fans stormed a club in [[Strøget]], composed of Arsenal supporters.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Flemming Emil |last1=Hansen |first2=Jakob |last2=Weiss |first3=Anders |last3=Wiig |title=Hooligans af værste caliber |url=http://www.b.dk/danmark/hooligans-af-vaerste-kaliber |work=Berlingske Tidende |date=19 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013 |language=da}}</ref> Arsenal fans responded by provoking the Galatasaray followers, along with fans of other clubs involved, as retribution for the two Leeds United supporters murdered, before the club's semi-final first leg match against Galatasaray.<ref>{{cite news |first=Denis |last=Campbell |title=Soldiers join hooligans to attack Turks in Cup clash |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2000/may/14/uefa.football |work=The Guardian |date=14 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> Four Britons and Turks were apprehended by the Danish riot police following the violence.<ref>{{cite news |title=English, Turkish fans clash on eve final |url=http://espn.go.com/soccer/news/2000/0516/535506.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140202175359/http://espn.go.com/soccer/news/2000/0516/535506.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 February 2014 |publisher=ESPN (Entertainment and Sports Programming Network) |agency=Associated Press |date=16 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> The turmoil was covered by some British media; tabloid newspaper the ''[[Daily Mirror]]'', published images, believed to be the Arsenal fans involved in the onslaught,<ref>{{cite news |title=Faces of Hatred |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/FACES+OF+HATRED.-a062192124 |work=Daily Mirror |date=19 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> while [[BBC News]], [[ITN|ITN News]] and [[Sky News]] reported and broadcast television footage of the riots.<ref>{{cite AV media |date=17 May 2000 |title=UEFA Cup Final: Violence |medium=Video clip |url=http://www.itnsource.com/shotlist/ITN/2000/05/17/BSP170500002/BSP170500002-0 |access-date=28 July 2013 |work=ITN Source |publisher=Independent Television News}}</ref>

One Arsenal supporter, Paul Dineen was stabbed in the back with a knife, during the riots in a pub, near the [[City Hall Square, Copenhagen|City Hall Square]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Teargas fired on fighting fans |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/751924.stm |work=BBC News |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> The incident caused Arsenal to offer their fans full compensation, if they did not want to travel and attend the match.<ref>{{cite news |title=English fan stabbed in Copenhagen |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/may/17/newsstory.sport3 |work=Theguardian.com |date=17 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> Another three members of the public, identified as one Englishman, Turk and Dutchman were also wounded by knifing when the event was still under its way.<ref>{{cite news |title=Three more stabbed in Copenhagen |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/752596.stm |work=BBC News |publisher=BBC |date=18 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref> Sixty people, all presumed to have been involved, were detained by Danish law enforcement, while another 19 suffered injuries as a result of the violence.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Vivek |last1=Chaudhary |first2=Jamie |last2=Wilson |title=Turkish and English fans clash at airport |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2000/may/19/uefa.football1 |work=The Guardian |date=19 May 2000 |access-date=28 July 2013}}</ref>

